      "fact_error": true,
      "notes": "Fact error in hint 2 and in Why not the others (C). Both claim 'solemnize' only means to perform a ceremony, which is wrong: standard dictionaries also give a second sense, 'to make serious or grave', and that is the sense the option 'solemnizing' is trading on here (a note that turns grave and formal). Hint 2 says: '\"Solemnizing\" means performing a ceremony. It is not a tone a poem ends on, so it is out.' The solution repeats the same claim: 'C. solemnizing: this means performing a ceremony, not a tone the poem ends on.' Because the definition given is incomplete, the reasoning that the word 'is not a tone a poem ends on' is false, since 'making grave/serious' plainly can describe an ending's tone. The elimination should rest on the poem's actual ending (hopeful, not grave), not on a false claim that the word cannot name a tone at all.",
      "fix": "Hint 2: \"'Solemnizing' would mean the ending turns grave and serious. Neto's closing lines are hopeful, not grave, so it is out.\"\n\nWhy not the others (C): \"C. solemnizing: this would mean the ending turns grave and serious; Neto's closing lines look forward with hope, not gravity.\"",
